551534, -0.024496714, -0.02423988, 0.015072727, 0.032169387, -0.031811822, -0.052332375, -0.020105744, 0.0020623524, -0.04296783, 0.020731838, -0.04849795, 0.011933715, 0.028871527, -0.033304922, -0.046025276, -0.13378292, -0.02267671, -0.05132013, -0.021892555, 0.058960915, 0.012112351, 0.013724995, 0.0061895503, 0.083121106, -0.18543933, -0.048372205, 0.0016244942, 0.018597528, 0.04686324, 0.12694374, -0.007996808, -0.075288735, 0.034813937, -0.048891228, -0.0050162678, -0.0053196712, 0.016292876, -0.07416519, 0.012777736, 0.0552112, -0.014178373, -0.0037106576, 0.0273857, -0.10198839, -0.042102586, 0.04573151, 0.11452747, 0.023987927, 0.10538768, 0.00352485, 0.017063752, 0.024998678, -0.02867651, 0.03359103, 0.09147665, -0.033399705, 0.022771588, 0.060869735, 0.035218835, 0.011149419, 0.06948814, 0.006648963, 0.050769307, -0.013738481, 0.09712749, -0.017936783, 0.009793913, 0.054010235, 0.123485364, -0.037894335, 0.016314749, 0.04455598, -0.020372517, -0.018687613, -0.016759071, -0.011881592, 0.053238284, 0.004857962, 0.025282271, -0.046640348, -0.008080645, 0.020627396, -0.03300923, 0.0037153657, 0.017255062, -0.07688383, -0.06468926, 0.023586327, 0.016907249], embedding_model: 'snowflake-arctic-embed-xs@d8c86521', source: source:`ontario_field_crop_diseases`, text: 'Incidence: The most common and important ear mould in Ontario is Gibberella zeae, which is the sexual reproductive stage of Fusarium graminearum. This fungus not only infects corn but also small grains such as wheat. Many plant pathologists believe that in years with a high occurrence of fusarium head blight in wheat, the potential exists for increased gibberella ear rot in corn.\nAppearance: Although the fungus can produce a white mould that makes it difficult to tell apart from fusarium ear rot, the two can be distinguished easily when Gibberella produces its characteristic red or dark pink (purple) mould (Photo 16–12).\nDisease Cycle: Infection begins through the silk channel and thus, in most cases starts at the ear tip and works its way down the ear. In severe cases, most of the ear may be covered with mould growth. Corn silks are most susceptible 2–10 days after initiation, and cool, wet weather during this period is ideal for infection.', text_hash: 'c8b67d7b4e1293f8614b22b3cfbadd2013873a1079cd63b582d1b2fbdb61ee16', topic: 'conditions' } RETURN NONE;
