5585139, -0.00010344661, 0.028871497, 0.023801414, -0.056835234, -0.022653388, -0.035060104, 0.042598825, 0.022085037, -0.042103246, -0.007684308, -0.00040012912, -0.038521394, -0.01934189, -0.120139204, -0.01012909, -0.0075190156, -0.023075933, 0.052447192, -0.044866726, 0.06038386, -0.017541388, 0.025912661, -0.18954074, -0.0019045155, 0.016613524, 0.03200589, 0.0053763087, 0.10153967, -0.0072159665, -0.11824704, 0.044294268, -0.0011748956, 0.012154236, 0.024693051, -0.029202117, -0.056329038, 0.0067924885, 0.08856224, -0.036309864, 0.045193, 0.050459784, -0.08037224, 0.029699113, 0.014710346, 0.17581657, 0.020528985, 0.022450112, 0.01827838, 0.0002941688, 0.01328487, -0.049867548, 0.02235947, 0.048583914, -0.042534817, -0.011185207, 0.06439316, 0.007953531, -0.023670403, 0.0564889, 0.008117756, 0.03540093, -0.011605197, 0.11625748, -0.0062188176, -0.005471228, 0.015914712, 0.04315161, -0.06407474, -0.039857212, -0.032069113, -0.03432804, 0.02504017, -0.018848533, -0.0024922327, -0.031093884, -0.012034332, 0.024412928, 0.016060654, -0.0039869435, -0.014145287, -0.017415015, 0.05000891, -0.045368634, -0.013134947, -0.047863252, -0.05066794, -0.027619084], embedding_model: 'snowflake-arctic-embed-xs@d8c86521', source: source:`ontario_field_crop_diseases`, text: 'Refer to the general seedling disease section at the beginning of the chapter.\nDisease Cycle: In corn, the most common diseases are caused by Pythium, Fusarium, Gibberella, Trichoderma and Penicillium, but other fungi such as Diplodia and Rhizoctonia can also be involved. Seed, seedling and roots infected by Pythium are most often soft (wet) and dark coloured, as opposed to roots infected with Fusarium, Gibberella, Diplodia and Rhizoctonia, which are firm or leathery. The colour of the roots most often provides a good indication of which organism(s) are present: greyish-white indicates Diplodia, tan to pink indicates Fusarium or Gibberella, reddish to brown indicates Rhizoctonia and blue-green indicates Penicillium or Trichoderma.\nPythium, Fusarium, Gibberella, Diplodia, Rhizoctonia, Penicillium and Trichoderma all live and thrive in the soil. In most cases, they can affect other crops beside corn. Except for Pythium, all of these organisms also have the ability to live on or in corn seed.', text_hash: '14385de68ccf0d5ff74e52aea89048f02697258b63e3b804bf532a705352c97a', topic: 'conditions' } RETURN NONE;
