9, 0.057731066, 0.00364238, -0.043954838, 0.021412626, -0.052467268, 0.008857654, 0.009648047, -0.021398112, -0.033996455, 0.011755614, -0.03678355, 0.05443008, 0.053691912, 0.0043767644, -0.023039175, -0.059217162, -0.034571603, 0.003352731, 0.042644385, 0.03228102, -0.0390246, -0.011140163, -0.029509632, 0.020344302, -0.145775, -0.0109591745, 0.034329224, 0.0138511965, 0.019508146, -0.014299773, 0.07020976, 0.042466026, 0.046856064, -0.0258125, 0.08134193, -0.012461596, 0.042320013, -0.007552767, 0.03271014, 0.08952856, -0.037208937, 0.034114018, 0.027627956, -0.0921135, -0.0074482556, 0.029989306, 0.12536924, -0.008944649, 0.029286452, -0.035810187, 0.032594535, 0.035255756, -0.058695637, 0.0051483903, 0.035089497, -0.04807775, 0.017537527, -0.01086493, -0.052297626, -0.09276526, 0.007833321, 0.00815822, 0.01965009, -0.038988765, 0.1019385, -0.007958043, -0.01887221, -0.023333993, -0.0042974297, -0.074415185, -0.015794652, -0.021445734, 0.030271601, 0.032850534, 0.004857307, 0.027706431, -0.009922326, 0.0011652387, 0.005415102, 0.064119324, 0.013834983, 0.021093134, 0.10152266, 0.01079216, -0.019029371, -0.07078805, -0.012101526, -0.06091682, 0.011251456], embedding_model: 'snowflake-arctic-embed-xs@d8c86521', source: source:`ontario_field_crops_soil_management`, text: 'Applying compost to the soil is another way of adding organic matter. Compost is blended from a variety of waste material inputs, which results in varying amounts of readily available nutrients. Leaf-yard sourced compost will supply relatively low amounts of readily available nutrients, while compost sourced from food waste, as well as composted poultry manure, will supply more nutrients. The composting process relies on specific moisture content and carbon-to-nitrogen ratio to allow micro-organisms to partially decompose organic matter, so the organic matter added to the soil is made up of more resistant compounds than in fresh manure. Micro-organisms in the soil utilize the compounds so that nutrient cycling in the soil releases nutrients to crops over a longer period of time.\nComposted materials combined with applications of fresh organic residues (manure or cover crops) will stimulate production of compounds that act as a glue (called glomalins) to hold aggregates together. Compost should not be the only source of organic matter, as soils benefit from fresh residues as well. Fresh residues will stimulate more production of the sticky material that holds aggregates together than compost. Similar to manure, it is important to minimize compaction at application and avoid excessive nutrient additions.', text_hash: 'f8e96098657462cf90ad7d6452f10fc8b48dd6199dd965ccbcd9d7fd2ad289af', topic: 'care' } RETURN NONE;
