3306, 0.041460622, -0.04965878, 0.047272276, -0.0046221414, -0.006476568, -0.057021458, -0.03038178, -0.06410068, 0.021476256, -0.031019216, 0.05742121, 0.028057031, -0.03209784, 0.0070645097, -0.09461246, 0.004114221, -0.0462741, 0.016587302, 0.080238536, -0.0105383545, 0.068256974, -0.04597796, 0.004666004, -0.12261829, 0.015212834, 0.042847473, 0.072661534, -0.0001714969, -0.001818874, 0.015024743, 0.028474083, 0.040922742, -0.03302242, 0.05599746, -0.025557779, 0.0011522872, -0.0324397, -0.00020550287, 0.05876929, -0.04013791, 0.039539583, 0.02799201, -0.10925833, 0.048234016, 0.020505745, 0.08494888, 0.027808473, 0.06623951, -0.017001515, -0.020113654, -0.008990638, -0.10062565, 0.03320163, -0.010099083, -0.036686894, 0.020103414, 0.018655162, 0.015320322, -0.043283723, 0.07447699, 0.0025717902, 0.02123171, -0.032654643, 0.1507327, 0.0046675038, 0.016415356, 0.023369806, 0.061491422, -0.06446335, -0.019573757, -0.022251036, -0.01373951, 0.018764261, -0.009422571, -0.0046742843, 0.000047690308, 0.013981084, 0.00720005, 0.06002091, -0.015585108, 0.02639898, 0.089507826, 0.003185683, 0.0077935546, 0.0015614228, -0.004087357, -0.028739322, -0.09030754], embedding_model: 'snowflake-arctic-embed-xs@d8c86521', source: source:`ontario_field_crops_soil_management`, text: 'Importance Soil life drives residue breakdown, nutrient cycling and organic matter stabilization (through aggregation). Macropores created by larger organisms aid in water movement through the soil. Organic matter plays a key role in the development of soil structure. A lifeless soil will not be productive.\nAssessment •  Count the number of large earthworm holes (middens, as shown in the Photo 8–5a in a square metre. A good population is 10 or more per m2. •  Small earthworm populations can be assessed by digging up a shovelful of soil and breaking it apart to see how many earthworms are found. Earthworms help build soil structure and have a role in water movement through the soil (Photos 8–5a and 8–5b). The smell of a soil can also be an indicator of a healthy soil life population. A sweet, forest smell is good; a swampy smell indicates a less ideal situation.', text_hash: '7b7ba6b401a46f6d8ad955a690efb9fa51b7793473223f1ac703ab51db1e559f', topic: 'care' } RETURN NONE;
